---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Postscript vs Attentive for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Postscript vs Attentive for Shopify stores — SMS acquisition, reply ownership, email scope, and plan-level procurement tests.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Postscript or Attentive better for Shopify SMS?',
    answer: 'Use Shopify SMS workflow fit, staffing and a matched quote to decide. Test cart events, keyword capture and reply routing in each candidate account. Revenue alone does not establish a suitable platform or contract tier; confirm current integrations, permissions and support scope before selection.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Attentive replace Postscript on Shopify?',
    answer: 'Treat replacement as a migration to prove in a test account. Verify supported Shopify events, consent evidence, suppression imports, historical data and reply routing. Assign one recovery sender, cancel old queued work and replay an event to confirm deduplication before retiring the previous workflow.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Postscript vs Attentive pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Request current written quotes for the same twelve-month workload, including a proposed 60k-subscriber flash sale. Count SMS segments, MMS, carrier and destination fees, minimum commitments, overages, onboarding, support, reply handling and any separately selected email service. A subscriber count alone cannot establish the cheaper option.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has stronger TCPA and compliance tooling?',
    answer: 'Assess tooling against your documented requirements rather than treating either vendor as a compliance guarantee. Verify consent evidence by purpose and channel, opt-out propagation, quiet-hour configuration, role access and exportable audit records in the proposed plan. Obtain qualified review of the actual program; vendor positioning cannot settle legal suitability.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy with Postscript or Attentive?',
    answer: 'Both vendors offer email as well as SMS. Confirm account access and test your required email journeys before adding another sender. Consider Sequenzy or another email service only for a documented unmet requirement, with integration, permission, queue ownership and total cost acceptance; no pairing is required by category.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles two-way SMS and conversational commerce better?',
    answer: 'Test both candidates with a sizing question, an order-edit request and a VIP reply. Measure routing accuracy, human handoff, queue ownership, response staffing and duplicate replies. Confirm whether an order change requires a separate Shopify action and which team owns it. These are proposed checks, not measured implementation times.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Attentive beat Postscript for list growth?',
    answer: 'Use a proposed ninety-day capture fixture with eighteen thousand SMS signups as a planning target, not a merchant result. Compare keyword, popup, QR and in-store entry paths only where currently supported. Record channel-specific permission, duplicate identities, confirmed opt-ins, suppression and retained engaged subscribers; raw signup totals do not establish a winner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Propose four tests: a keyword opt-in from a Shopify popup, cart recovery across quiet hours, a simulated broadcast audience of 30k+ eligible subscribers, and a two-way product question. At dispatch recheck purpose and channel permission, suppression, purchase state and queue ownership. Cancel stale recovery and replay events to prove deduplication. Compare attributed orders separately from a randomized holdout estimate with uncertainty, then model matched all-in cost.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Postscript vs Attentive</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Attentive for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          Two SMS candidates can be compared through proposed Shopify fixtures: board game allocation drops, outdoor retail procurement requirements and tea replenishment. These are test scenarios, not observed merchant results. Confirm current plan capabilities and contract terms before relying on the vendor positioning below.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Postscript', tool: 'SMS, RCS and Email', reason: 'Verify channel access, identity handling and required Shopify events in your offered account.' },
            { label: 'Attentive', tool: 'Email, SMS and service scope', reason: 'Verify journey coverage and whether Concierge or other reply support is included in the proposal.' },
            { label: 'Decision', tool: 'Accepted workflow and quote', reason: 'Select against the same fixtures, reply staffing, dispatch controls and seasonal workload.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Attentive</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Account access</td><td>Confirm Email access and SMS/RCS availability for your account</td><td>Confirm Email/SMS modules and offered service scope</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Acquisition and identity</td><td>Replay keyword and popup signup; inspect profile merges</td><td>Replay the same paths; inspect profile merges</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reply ownership</td><td>Demonstrate response management and human escalation</td><td>Demonstrate reply routing; specify Concierge access if proposed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Procurement</td><td>Itemize channel fees, minimums, onboarding and support</td><td>Itemize the same workload, service fees and commitments</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Governance</td><td>Export permission history and demonstrate role restrictions</td><td>Demonstrate the same restrictions and evidence exports</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Launch readiness</td><td>Approve payloads, suppression, cancellation and operator handoff</td><td>Meet the same acceptance gates before cutover</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What this comparison is actually about</h2>
        <p>
          This comparison focuses on SMS acquisition, recovery and conversations within a Shopify lifecycle stack. Specify any separate email service and operational notification owner. Neither category placement nor a sales demonstration proves a current capability. Test the exact plan, event payloads and staffing arrangement you intend to operate.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Current product scope and account acceptance</h2>
        <p>
          Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">Postscript announces Email</a> alongside SMS and RCS; its announcement links to an interest list, so confirm access rather than assuming every existing account has it. The <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/rcs">RCS page</a> describes phased early access. <a class="link" href="https://www.attentive.com/email-marketing">Attentive offers Email</a> and cross-channel journeys. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4460351735060-Get-started-with-Attentive-Concierge">Concierge documentation</a> describes live-agent SMS conversations using merchant service materials. Product availability does not establish included service, account eligibility or comparative performance.
        </p>
        <h2>One dispatch and identity contract for all six fixtures</h2>
        <p>
          Name one sender and queue owner per job and channel. Prioritize required service updates, eligible recovery, education and promotions according to the merchant's rules; priority grants no marketing permission. Record signup source, purpose, channel, timestamp and suppression evidence. Merge a duplicate phone/email identity without transferring permission between channels. At dispatch recheck permission, purchase state and eligibility; cancel stale jobs and replay the event identifier to prove deduplication. Inject delayed suppression and measure propagation latency, including the pending queue, rather than assuming a forty-five-minute delay is safe. Keep service responses and marketing eligibility separate.
        </p>
        <h2>Pottery Studio: class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the pottery studio handles 480 orders/mo at roughly $31k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets, which means email must handle kiln-care post-purchase and holiday gift exclusions before holiday starter-set rush. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: exclude class waitlist leads from glaze-kit promos after signup source tagging.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the pottery fixture, keep class waitlist permission separate from glaze-kit promotions. Test signup source tagging, a kiln-care purchase and a holiday gift exclusion. Suppress ineligible promotions when a class lead has no product-marketing permission; route service updates through their designated owner.
        </p>
        <h2>How vinyl record shop emails through Record Store Day</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the vinyl record shop handles 1,800 orders/mo at roughly $72k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows limited pressings with 200–400 unit runs, which means email must handle VIP drop allocation and grading education before promos before Record Store Day. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: suppress recent full-price buyers from instant discount blasts on scarce pressings.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the vinyl fixture, use a proposed 300-unit pressing during a two-hour send window. Simulate sold-out variants, full-price purchases and VIP allocation changes. Recheck availability and eligibility at dispatch, cancel obsolete discounts and record how stale links are handled without claiming recovered margin.
        </p>
        <h2>Outdoor Gear Retailer ($112k/mo) — merchandise ultralight versus family car-camping with different hero tents</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the outdoor gear retailer handles 2,400 orders/mo at roughly $112k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows layering guides and ultralight segment tags, which means email must handle Memorial Day tent promos only after education sends before Memorial Day sale week. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: merchandise ultralight versus family car-camping with different hero tents.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the outdoor fixture, compare ultralight and family car-camping segments using different hero tents. Simulate a sold-out tent and a purchase after education but before recovery. Verify product relevance, current inventory and suppression; an old browse event must not authorize another reminder.
        </p>
        <h2>Before GB close deadlines: mechanical keyboard vendor retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the mechanical keyboard vendor handles 940 orders/mo at roughly $58k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows six-week group-buy cycles on $180–$420 kits, which means email must handle GB close suppression and switch-type education across five PDPs before GB close deadlines. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: halt cart recovery when preorder tags flip to closed.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the keyboard fixture, use a proposed ten-day consideration period and a six-hour weekly operator-effort budget. Test switch education across five PDPs and closure of a group buy. Measure effort rather than claiming hours saved, and cancel queued cart recovery when preorder state closes.
        </p>
        <h2>Board Game Store: pre-order allocation anxiety on hot titles</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the board game store handles 1,050 orders/mo at roughly $67k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows pre-order allocation anxiety on hot titles, which means email must handle pre-order state machines and fulfillment pivots after allocation closes before Essen release week. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: pause winback until fulfillment tags show shipped for pre-orders.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the board game fixture, test allocation pending, confirmed, cancelled and shipped states. Pause winback until the correct fulfillment state permits it, and deduplicate repeated allocation events. A delayed cart event must not trigger recovery after purchase or after the allocation closes.
        </p>
        <h2>How specialty tea merchant emails through Q4 gift-box peak</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed fixture, the specialty tea merchant handles 720 orders/mo at roughly $44k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows 28–35 day tin replenishment with gift-box seasonality, which means email must handle subscription skip handling and gift-buyer exclusions in December before Q4 gift-box peak. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: split replenishment windows by blend and steeping rate.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the tea fixture, model blend-specific 28–35 day replenishment with subscription active, skipped, paused and cancelled states. Separate gift recipients and gift buyers from self-purchasers. Recheck subscription and purchase state before dispatch; a historical average does not establish current eligibility.
        </p>
        <h2>Reply and service ownership acceptance</h2>
        <p>
          Treat SMS ownership, supported event coverage and operator workload as decision criteria. Confirm reply staffing, approval requirements and audit exports in each quoted plan. Use the merchant workload below to size staffing; no order or revenue threshold determines vendor suitability. An order-edit reply must reach an authorized Shopify operator, while a product question may remain with the messaging team. Test an unavailable agent, escalation and a closed support ticket; record who can resume marketing after a service hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          Model a real annual calendar with quiet months and peak broadcasts. Include thousands of proposed waitlist imports without assuming they become eligible SMS subscribers. Confirm what each quote bills for contacts, segments, destinations, carrier fees, support and minimums; no merchant billing outcome has been measured here.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Newsletter layer: editorial merchandising without discount training</h2>
        <p>
          Use vinyl staff picks, keyboard switch explainers and outdoor trip reports as proposed email merchandising jobs. Name the email sender, which may be the same vendor as SMS, and confirm any required integration. Test inventory changes during a campaign and prevent competing recovery queues from contacting the same shopper after purchase.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Pricing models that punish different mistakes</h2>
        <p>
          Use 12,000–18,000 contacts as a proposed quoting fixture, distinguishing eligible SMS subscribers from email-only contacts and suppressed profiles. Include required modules, integration work, migration, support labor and separate email costs only where that architecture is selected. Do not assume plans converge or that optional modules are included.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Hybrid stack patterns on Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          First test whether the selected vendor can own the required email and SMS journeys. A split stack may retain a capture tool or separate email service where a specific requirement fails acceptance. Verify current integration support and data flow. Assign cart recovery priority across all senders, cancel queued messages after purchase or opt-out, and replay the same event identifier to verify that only one eligible job remains.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Record an acceptance decision for either vendor</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Procurement: obtain a written scope for channels, destinations, minimums, renewals, exit terms, migration and support. Set the launch date from completed dependencies, not a vendor-wide timetable.</li>
          <li>Operations: retain payload and dispatch logs for the relevant fixture, including rejection reasons, queue cancellations and reply escalation. Have the weekly operator reproduce an audience change without vendor intervention.</li>
          <li>Economics: model quiet and peak months over twelve months. Add recurring platform, usage, carrier, service and labor costs; report migration and overlap separately. Count eligible subscribers and billable units rather than treating every stored profile as sendable.</li>
          <li>Selection: choose Postscript or Attentive only when its offered account passes required jobs and its service arrangement is staffed. If both pass, compare costs and experimental evidence; if neither passes, retain the working workflow while resolving the failed requirement.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Shortlist both against the required SMS jobs and ownership model. Separate attributed revenue from incremental profit: use a randomized holdout where feasible, report uncertainty and account for discounts, returns, messaging fees and labor. Run the four-job FAQ test in both UIs, model twelve-month billing with real sends and list hygiene, and pick whichever platform lets you edit audiences safely the Thursday before a drop — not whichever sales deck had the prettier logos. See <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models</a> before annual contracts.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive">Omnisend vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump">Postscript vs SMSBump</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for">Browse store-type guides</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
